<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jahangir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1569">1569</a>–<a href="/years/1627">1627</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Prince Salim</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jahangir was the fourth Mughal emperor, reigning from 1605 to 1627. The son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, born Prince Salim, he largely kept his father's system of government, made peace with Mewar in 1615 and received the English envoy Sir Thomas Roe. A connoisseur of painting and nature, he wrote a candid memoir, while his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-jahan">Nur Jahan</a> held great influence at court.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jahangir.jpg" alt="Jahangir" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abu al-Hasan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jahangircrop.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The relationship with the Mughal Empire was defined by the treaty of 1615, when Amar Singh I accepted <b>Jahangir</b>’s overlordship through Prince Khurram; Mewar was spared the marriage alliances made by other Rajput houses, the Maharana was excused personal attendance at court and his heir served in his…</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The growing community attracted the suspicion of the Mughal authorities, and in 1606 Guru Arjan was executed under the emperor <b>Jahangir</b>, becoming the first Sikh martyr.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In law the emperor was the final judge: <b>Jahangir</b> hung a “chain of justice” in the Agra Fort that any wronged subject could shake.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mughal-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Kishangarh was founded in 1611 by Kishan Singh, a son of Raja Udai Singh of Marwar, who received the territory near Ajmer from the Mughal emperor <b>Jahangir</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kishangarh-state">Kishangarh State</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 16th century they moved their seat into the hills at Dhameri, which was renamed Nurpur in the early 17th century after the emperor <b>Jahangir</b>, Nur-ud-din.</blockquote><a href="/state/nurpur-state">Nurpur State</a></li><li><blockquote>Bir Singh Deo had Akbar's minister Abul Fazl killed in 1602 on behalf of <b>Prince Salim</b>, the future Jahangir, and was rewarded with power and wealth; he built the Jahangir Mahal and many temples.</blockquote><a href="/state/orchha-state">Orchha State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatehpur-sikri">Fatehpur Sikri</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Rajauri, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.09,77.66&amp;year=1606" aria-label="Fatehpur Sikri on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6593 1170 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jahangir"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7043 1440h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7043 1440h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=27.09,77.66&amp;year=1606">What was here in 1606? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;After my accession, the first order that I gave was for the fastening up of the Chain of Justice…&#8221;</p><footer>Tuzuk-i-Jahangiri (his memoirs), on his accession, 1605 &#183; tr. Alexander Rogers, ed. Henry Beveridge, 1909 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/53674/pg53674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…although I myself drink wine, and from the age of 18 years up till now, when I am 38, have persisted in it.&#8221;</p><footer>Tuzuk-i-Jahangiri (his memoirs), his edict against making wine &#183; tr. Alexander Rogers, ed. Henry Beveridge, 1909 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/53674/pg53674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The good qualities of my revered father are beyond the limit of approval and the bounds of praise.&#8221;</p><footer>Tuzuk-i-Jahangiri (his memoirs), on his father &#183; tr. Alexander Rogers, ed. Henry Beveridge, 1909 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/53674/pg53674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
